1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,840
Then we're going to talk about midterm.

2
00:00:06,740 --> 00:00:10,220
And then if we run out of things to talk about midterm,

3
00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:11,720
we are going to start week eight,

4
00:00:12,100 --> 00:00:13,600
although week eight will not be on the midterm.

5
00:00:17,780 --> 00:00:18,980
Don't want to have any dead time.

6
00:00:20,260 --> 00:00:20,560
All right.

7
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:22,180
Can we buy that dead time from you?

8
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:24,940
Can we buy that dead time from you with something?

9
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:26,140
Wait a second.

10
00:00:26,280 --> 00:00:26,740
No, no, no.

11
00:00:26,860 --> 00:00:27,940
You shouldn't.

12
00:00:27,940 --> 00:00:29,820
I should be buying dead time from you.

13
00:00:29,820 --> 00:00:31,140
You shouldn't want dead time from me.

14
00:00:31,140 --> 00:00:33,440
Good. So what would you pay us so that we can go home

15
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:34,280
if we're done with it?

16
00:00:34,280 --> 00:00:39,020
What is your income from substitution?

17
00:00:39,020 --> 00:00:42,740
Anyway, here, I'm very confused by this offer.

18
00:00:43,140 --> 00:00:43,620
All right.

19
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:46,060
Okay. All right.

20
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:49,000
So in the last class, we were just doing a review

21
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:50,960
of public goods and externalities

22
00:00:51,480 --> 00:00:56,880
with my slightly eccentric sociological theory

23
00:00:56,880 --> 00:00:58,640
of when economists actually switch

24
00:00:58,640 --> 00:01:01,020
between talking about positive externalities

25
00:01:01,020 --> 00:01:03,600
of public goods, negative externalities of public goods.

26
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:04,680
This is not in any textbook.

27
00:01:05,080 --> 00:01:07,180
This is just for observing how economists

28
00:01:07,180 --> 00:01:10,000
in fact use the work, right, or use the terms.

29
00:01:10,660 --> 00:01:10,880
All right.

30
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:14,800
So my suggestion is first of all, for thinking

31
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:17,220
about positive externalities, of course,

32
00:01:17,320 --> 00:01:18,900
one way you can diagram them.

33
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:22,040
This is not the only way, but one way you can do it is just

34
00:01:22,040 --> 00:01:25,720
to show a social benefits curve above the demand curve,

35
00:01:25,720 --> 00:01:27,780
indicating that there are these extra benefits

36
00:01:27,780 --> 00:01:28,780
for the bystanders.

37
00:01:29,340 --> 00:01:31,180
And then we can think about public goods

38
00:01:31,720 --> 00:01:35,180
as just being an extreme case of positive externalities.

39
00:01:35,660 --> 00:01:39,420
So one, we've got supply up here, social benefits up here,

40
00:01:39,420 --> 00:01:41,100
and then demand somewhere around there.

41
00:01:41,660 --> 00:01:43,620
Of course, this is a fuzzy concept

42
00:01:43,620 --> 00:01:45,160
when you're applying it to the real world.

43
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:48,780
But again, you might think about something like national defense

44
00:01:48,780 --> 00:01:51,880
for Switzerland during World War II, how many people are going

45
00:01:51,880 --> 00:01:54,920
to go and voluntarily pay money for the Swiss defense?

46
00:01:55,640 --> 00:01:57,000
Right? Probably very few.

47
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:00,400
And yet, how good is it for Switzerland to not get invaded

48
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:02,040
by Nazi Germany during World War II?

49
00:02:02,260 --> 00:02:02,840
Very good.

50
00:02:03,020 --> 00:02:03,740
That's very good.

51
00:02:03,740 --> 00:02:06,080
In fact, it's even really good for the soldiers

52
00:02:06,080 --> 00:02:09,240
because it's way better to drink cocoa in the Alps

53
00:02:09,380 --> 00:02:13,060
in a Swiss uniform than to be dying on the steps of Russia

54
00:02:13,060 --> 00:02:14,760
in a German military uniform.

55
00:02:14,760 --> 00:02:17,460
And probably most of those Swiss guys just would have been put

56
00:02:17,460 --> 00:02:19,340
into German uniforms, declared to be German,

57
00:02:19,820 --> 00:02:21,800
and sent to fight in Russia,

58
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:24,060
which is really bad from what I've heard.

59
00:02:24,340 --> 00:02:24,440
Right?

60
00:02:24,440 --> 00:02:28,900
So there is a standard threat in all World War II movies.

61
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:30,060
I'll send you to the Eastern Front.

62
00:02:30,060 --> 00:02:31,400
No, no. All right.

63
00:02:31,780 --> 00:02:32,000
All right.

64
00:02:32,680 --> 00:02:35,300
So anyway, this is, as I'm drawing it,

65
00:02:35,300 --> 00:02:38,260
just an extreme version of positive externalities.

66
00:02:38,780 --> 00:02:40,800
And then similarly for negative externalities,

67
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:41,980
we just flip things around.

68
00:02:41,980 --> 00:02:43,080
So here we have supply,

69
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:45,380
here we have demand and social benefits curves.

70
00:02:45,380 --> 00:02:46,720
And this is below the demand curve.

71
00:02:47,260 --> 00:02:48,960
This is for negative externalities.

72
00:02:49,520 --> 00:02:52,260
And then, if we're talking about public goods,

73
00:02:52,260 --> 00:02:54,180
then we can just think of this as a situation

74
00:02:54,180 --> 00:02:56,940
where there is quite a bit of demand,

75
00:02:56,940 --> 00:02:59,580
but the social benefits are basically zero.

76
00:02:59,580 --> 00:03:03,240
So roughly socially optimal for the thing not to exist at all.

77
00:03:03,240 --> 00:03:05,980
And again, it's very tempting to say, well, isn't that true

78
00:03:05,980 --> 00:03:07,460
for all things negative externalities?

79
00:03:07,460 --> 00:03:08,820
And the answer, of course, is no.

80
00:03:08,820 --> 00:03:11,520
I mean, there are many things with negative externalities

81
00:03:11,520 --> 00:03:14,300
where if we just stop them from happening today,

82
00:03:14,580 --> 00:03:16,940
it would lead to terrible results.

83
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:19,620
Right? Like if you just stopped all emissions

84
00:03:19,620 --> 00:03:21,420
of internal combustion engines today.

85
00:03:21,420 --> 00:03:24,180
Beginning now, with no warning.

86
00:03:24,740 --> 00:03:27,260
This would probably starve most of mankind to death

87
00:03:27,260 --> 00:03:29,180
in a few months if you could really enforce the law.

88
00:03:29,780 --> 00:03:30,720
Right? Because no one is abandoning it.

89
00:03:30,960 --> 00:03:32,280
Passing a law preventing anyone

90
00:03:32,280 --> 00:03:33,920
from using an internal combustion engine does not

91
00:03:33,920 --> 00:03:36,440
magically give you an alternative way of growing

92
00:03:36,440 --> 00:03:37,420
and transporting food.

93
00:03:38,160 --> 00:03:39,840
Right? So that could happen eventually.

94
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:42,680
Right? But it's not going to happen overnight,

95
00:03:42,680 --> 00:03:43,980
unless you get really lucky.

96
00:03:43,980 --> 00:03:46,080
And you might not want to gamble the lives

97
00:03:46,080 --> 00:03:48,240
of most of mankind on, well, maybe.

98
00:03:48,240 --> 00:03:52,700
Maybe we're just a little bit away from this.

99
00:03:53,200 --> 00:03:53,720
All right?

100
00:03:53,720 --> 00:03:57,040
So we think about this as a situation of a public bad.

101
00:03:58,100 --> 00:04:01,000
Right? And again, so just an extremely extreme negative

102
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:01,640
externality.

103
00:04:01,640 --> 00:04:02,960
This is nonstandard.

104
00:04:02,960 --> 00:04:05,260
So if you go to a textbook, they'll talk a lot more

105
00:04:05,260 --> 00:04:08,300
about nonrivalry as being an additional trait.

106
00:04:08,680 --> 00:04:11,180
Although, if you really think about it, nonrivalry is

107
00:04:11,180 --> 00:04:12,580
so ubiquitous in the economy.

108
00:04:13,520 --> 00:04:17,200
Right? So any time you go to a matinee, you see nonrivalry.

109
00:04:17,200 --> 00:04:20,360
Right? How many of those seats are actually occupied,

110
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:22,000
even at the matinee price?

111
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:23,860
Right? So it's already reduced,

112
00:04:23,860 --> 00:04:25,500
and yet almost all the seats are empty.

113
00:04:25,840 --> 00:04:28,500
So you can say almost all those seats are nonrival.

114
00:04:28,780 --> 00:04:30,420
Or just think about whatever food gets thrown

115
00:04:30,420 --> 00:04:31,940
out of a restaurant at the end of the day.

116
00:04:32,480 --> 00:04:35,900
Right? Or you can even go and look at how many seats are empty

117
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:37,020
at restaurants most of the time.

118
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:39,740
And yet, it doesn't look

119
00:04:39,740 --> 00:04:42,040
like they are pricing anything close to marginal cost.

120
00:04:42,040 --> 00:04:44,280
They've got some, you know,

121
00:04:44,720 --> 00:04:46,600
because usually there's some reduction in price.

122
00:04:46,600 --> 00:04:48,200
But if you're wondering, well,

123
00:04:48,200 --> 00:04:51,660
like why would they not have cut price if they are not on,

124
00:04:51,660 --> 00:04:54,080
if they, why would they keep prices above marginal cost?

125
00:04:54,080 --> 00:04:57,580
And the obvious answer there is it's not for recompetition.

126
00:04:58,080 --> 00:04:59,960
Like if a movie theater were to cut the price by a penny,

127
00:04:59,960 --> 00:05:01,200
would all the seats fill instantly?

128
00:05:02,260 --> 00:05:03,320
No, right? And wait,

129
00:05:03,320 --> 00:05:05,000
what does the permitting competitive model imply would

130
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:05,500
happen?

131
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:10,160
That. It implies that if you cut your, the price of your seats

132
00:05:10,160 --> 00:05:11,620
by one penny below the market price,

133
00:05:11,860 --> 00:05:12,880
you could fill all your seats.

134
00:05:13,380 --> 00:05:15,700
And you may have noticed that that's not actually true.

135
00:05:15,700 --> 00:05:18,500
So, permitting competitive model, while useful

136
00:05:18,500 --> 00:05:21,500
for many purposes, is also wrong for a lot of other purposes.

137
00:05:23,200 --> 00:05:25,360
Okay. All right.

138
00:05:25,360 --> 00:05:27,340
So, just questions about this.

139
00:05:27,720 --> 00:05:28,400
I'm wondering.

140
00:05:28,980 --> 00:05:29,360
All right.

141
00:05:30,060 --> 00:05:32,160
Right now, a common observation that people make is

142
00:05:32,160 --> 00:05:35,760
that if you just think about the actors as being firms

143
00:05:35,760 --> 00:05:39,240
in an industry, then you could say that collusion is a public

144
00:05:39,240 --> 00:05:40,600
good for firms in an industry.

145
00:05:41,380 --> 00:05:42,320
Right? In some sense.

146
00:05:42,680 --> 00:05:44,920
Right? So, all the firms in the industry would be better off

147
00:05:44,920 --> 00:05:48,460
if they could all agree to raise prices for a certain quantity.

148
00:05:48,840 --> 00:05:50,720
But holding constant the behavior

149
00:05:50,720 --> 00:05:52,940
of all the other firms, no firm wants to do that.

150
00:05:54,080 --> 00:05:55,480
Right? So, standard story.

151
00:05:55,480 --> 00:05:57,760
Everybody wants to be the one firm that doesn't cut back.

152
00:05:58,400 --> 00:05:59,980
The one firm that doesn't raise prices.

153
00:06:01,020 --> 00:06:01,220
All right?

154
00:06:01,580 --> 00:06:04,780
So, we all suggest that we can go and, you know,

155
00:06:04,880 --> 00:06:07,900
use the provision of public goods using the same tools we've

156
00:06:07,900 --> 00:06:09,940
already been using for competition and collusion.

157
00:06:10,500 --> 00:06:13,520
So, let's think about a simple game theory model

158
00:06:13,520 --> 00:06:15,580
of general giving.

159
00:06:15,580 --> 00:06:15,720
All right?

160
00:06:15,720 --> 00:06:18,040
And if you're going to notice, we're going

161
00:06:18,040 --> 00:06:19,880
to make it look exactly like a Cornell game.

162
00:06:19,880 --> 00:06:22,580
It's going to look at least really similar

163
00:06:22,580 --> 00:06:23,460
to a Cornell game.

164
00:06:23,960 --> 00:06:25,880
So, imagine that individual utility

165
00:06:25,880 --> 00:06:28,060
for any one actor is going to be equal

166
00:06:28,060 --> 00:06:31,420
to their own personal consumption times D,

167
00:06:31,720 --> 00:06:34,020
where D is the sum of all the donations.

168
00:06:34,680 --> 00:06:36,700
Right? And of course, the donations are going

169
00:06:36,700 --> 00:06:39,500
to be your stuff minus whatever you consume.

170
00:06:40,060 --> 00:06:40,240
All right?

171
00:06:40,240 --> 00:06:42,520
So, let's assume that everybody starts going down at one.

172
00:06:42,520 --> 00:06:49,860
So, then donations will be equal to the sum of all the stuff

173
00:06:49,860 --> 00:06:51,060
that individuals don't consume.

174
00:06:51,560 --> 00:06:54,360
Right? So, 1 minus CI, that's the stuff you don't consume.

175
00:06:54,360 --> 00:06:56,260
And in a simple model, if you don't consume it,

176
00:06:56,260 --> 00:06:57,040
then what do you do?

177
00:06:57,040 --> 00:06:57,540
You donate.

178
00:06:57,540 --> 00:06:58,940
It's a one period model.

179
00:06:58,940 --> 00:07:00,800
So, what else would you do with the stuff?

180
00:07:01,080 --> 00:07:03,360
If you didn't consume it yourself, you would donate it.

181
00:07:03,800 --> 00:07:06,000
And then the sum of all the donations, of course,

182
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:08,860
will be the sum of all these, of all the stuff

183
00:07:08,860 --> 00:07:10,320
that people don't consume themselves.

184
00:07:10,320 --> 00:07:13,080
Right? Again, like I said, we're assuming everyone starts

185
00:07:13,080 --> 00:07:14,560
with an endowment of one to keep things simple.

186
00:07:15,160 --> 00:07:17,420
So, then if everybody's utility is going to be the product

187
00:07:17,420 --> 00:07:20,500
of their own consumption times all the endowments,

188
00:07:21,280 --> 00:07:23,520
times the sum of all the endowments that are donated,

189
00:07:24,140 --> 00:07:27,380
then we've got utility function that looks a lot like Cournot.

190
00:07:27,680 --> 00:07:29,060
Remember with Cournot, we've got this,

191
00:07:29,340 --> 00:07:32,400
we have your own Q times a P, which is a function

192
00:07:32,400 --> 00:07:34,240
of all the Qs that everybody's making.

193
00:07:34,740 --> 00:07:36,640
Remember what Cournot looks like?

194
00:07:37,120 --> 00:07:38,040
Remember? Yes, sir.

195
00:07:38,040 --> 00:07:41,000
You're maximizing with no cost, you're maximizing PQ,

196
00:07:41,240 --> 00:07:44,500
where P is a function of, you know, QQI, where P is a function

197
00:07:44,500 --> 00:07:46,700
of your own Q, but it's also primarily a function

198
00:07:46,700 --> 00:07:49,580
of all the other Qs of everybody other than yourself.

199
00:07:50,860 --> 00:07:52,240
Okay? All right?

200
00:07:52,600 --> 00:07:54,000
So, let me think of it this way.

201
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:56,980
So, CI plus DI equals 1.

202
00:07:58,020 --> 00:07:58,380
All right.

203
00:07:58,800 --> 00:08:00,740
And then we can go and solve this

204
00:08:00,740 --> 00:08:02,800
for asymmetric equilibrium.

205
00:08:03,500 --> 00:08:03,720
All right?

206
00:08:03,720 --> 00:08:06,320
So, again, all we do here is we can substitute that in.

207
00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:08,640
All right? And then we have, you know, your utility is going

208
00:08:08,640 --> 00:08:13,200
to be equal to your own consumption times the donations

209
00:08:13,200 --> 00:08:14,780
of everybody other than yourself.

210
00:08:15,080 --> 00:08:15,640
So, let's see.

211
00:08:15,840 --> 00:08:18,320
That should be, let's see.

212
00:08:18,520 --> 00:08:22,780
So, for all I not equal to J. Or, excuse me,

213
00:08:22,780 --> 00:08:23,500
you're actually on the way.

214
00:08:23,500 --> 00:08:27,920
We have all J not equal to I. J not equal to I. So,

215
00:08:27,920 --> 00:08:33,240
all the sum of the two Js plus and 1 minus CI.

216
00:08:33,740 --> 00:08:34,640
Right? That's your stuff.

217
00:08:34,640 --> 00:08:37,840
So, this here whole thing is equal to D. All right?

218
00:08:37,840 --> 00:08:41,040
So, that's all the donations of everybody besides yourself plus

219
00:08:41,040 --> 00:08:41,920
your own donation.

220
00:08:42,240 --> 00:08:43,460
Multiply that all through.

221
00:08:43,900 --> 00:08:47,700
Right? And if you go and work this out just

222
00:08:47,700 --> 00:08:49,120
like any other Cornell problem,

223
00:08:49,120 --> 00:08:52,700
you're going to get a pretty obvious result here.

224
00:08:52,700 --> 00:08:54,880
So, we're going to have, we can multiply it all together.

225
00:08:54,880 --> 00:08:58,760
Then we'll have CI times this plus another C

226
00:08:59,020 --> 00:09:00,440
and minus CI squared.

227
00:09:00,980 --> 00:09:02,380
Right? And what are we going to get?

228
00:09:02,380 --> 00:09:05,360
Well, so it's just like, it's just like Cornell.

229
00:09:05,360 --> 00:09:08,180
So, I'll let you work out the remaining bit of the math.

230
00:09:08,780 --> 00:09:11,020
But if we then do this and then the last step,

231
00:09:11,020 --> 00:09:12,660
remember with Cornell, is we look

232
00:09:12,660 --> 00:09:13,800
for the symmetric equilibrium.

233
00:09:14,920 --> 00:09:17,560
So, once everybody is doing the equilibrium thing,

234
00:09:18,160 --> 00:09:20,720
and since everybody's assumed to be the same, it would seem likely

235
00:09:20,720 --> 00:09:22,220
that everyone will wind up doing the same thing.

236
00:09:22,900 --> 00:09:25,320
Right? It seems to make sense.

237
00:09:25,760 --> 00:09:30,380
Then we get the solution to this game, which is, which is that C

238
00:09:30,380 --> 00:09:33,800
is going to be equal to N over N plus 1.

239
00:09:35,180 --> 00:09:39,620
Okay? Whereas, if you wanted to maximize social utility,

240
00:09:39,620 --> 00:09:44,300
you would just say that C is equal to.5 for all N.

241
00:09:44,360 --> 00:09:46,200
So, no matter how many actors there are,

242
00:09:46,580 --> 00:09:48,640
everywhere you always want to go and set,

243
00:09:48,860 --> 00:09:50,640
you consume half your own stuff

244
00:09:50,640 --> 00:09:51,840
and then donate half of your stuff.

245
00:09:53,020 --> 00:09:54,560
Right? And again, if you look at this,

246
00:09:54,560 --> 00:09:56,060
so suppose there's only one actor.

247
00:09:56,520 --> 00:09:58,020
So, is there going to be any externality problem

248
00:09:58,020 --> 00:10:00,480
with one actor?

249
00:10:00,880 --> 00:10:02,020
Do you generally have externalities

250
00:10:02,020 --> 00:10:02,780
when there's one person?

251
00:10:02,960 --> 00:10:04,420
No. All right.

252
00:10:04,420 --> 00:10:07,220
So, in that case, well, your optimal C is going

253
00:10:07,220 --> 00:10:10,680
to be the other individual C. The equilibrium will be 1 over 2,

254
00:10:10,680 --> 00:10:13,140
1 half, which is also the socially optimal thing.

255
00:10:13,700 --> 00:10:14,400
Does that make sense?

256
00:10:14,400 --> 00:10:16,120
So, when there's no one but yourself,

257
00:10:16,120 --> 00:10:17,320
you do the socially optimal thing.

258
00:10:17,660 --> 00:10:17,780
All right.

259
00:10:17,780 --> 00:10:18,920
Wow. All right.

260
00:10:19,700 --> 00:10:22,620
Right? That's the not so impressive invisible hand.

261
00:10:22,620 --> 00:10:25,200
If I am society, then I always do,

262
00:10:25,200 --> 00:10:26,600
and I always do what's best for me.

263
